  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-142876",
    "knowledge_point_id": "87675618-337f-5af2-8ad7-9132c080785a",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing a Conditional Probability",
    "problem": "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
    "worked_solution": "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=2)}{P(Y=2)}=\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(2) & =f(1,2)+f(2,2) \\\\  & =0.25+0.1 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(2,2)=0.1.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{Y}(2)=0.35$ and $f(2,2)=0.1$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1}{0.35} \\\\  & ≈0.29.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
